-- title:
--   The strong dual of a Hausdorff locally convex space is Hausdorff locally convex
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a Hausdorff locally convex topological vector space over $\mathbb C$, and let $E'_\beta$ denote its **strong dual**: the space of continuous linear functionals $f\colon E\to\mathbb C$ equipped with the topology of uniform convergence on the (von Neumann) bounded subsets of $E$. Then
--
--   $$E'_\beta\ \text{is a Hausdorff locally convex space.}$$
--
--   Indeed the strong topology is generated by the seminorms $p_B(f)=\sup_{x\in B}|f(x)|$, $B\subset E$ bounded, and it separates points because every singleton is bounded.
--
--   This is the basic structural fact needed before any permanence property (such as nuclearity) can be asked of the strong dual.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The strong dual is Mathlib's `StrongDual ℂ E`; the conclusion is the mission predicate `IsHLCS`, i.e. the topology is given by a family of seminorms and is $T_2$.
-- source:
--   H. H. Schaefer, Topological Vector Spaces (2nd ed.), Ch. III §3 (𝔖-topologies) and IV.5 (strong dual β(E',E)); A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes (draft 2026), Appendix C, §C.1.4 and Lemma C.2.4.11.

import Definitions.Def_MasulloGRH_LocallyConvex

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace MasulloGRH

theorem strongDual_isHLCS (E : Type*) [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℂ E]
    [TopologicalSpace E] (hE : IsHLCS E) :
    IsHLCS (StrongDual ℂ E) := by sorry

end MasulloGRH
